From: Local build <local@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] EXP: window: apply an interactive move at most twice per frame (KWIN_MOVE_COALESCE)

During an interactive move KWin moved the window at every pointer event: the next geometry (snap
zones, the visible-area confinement, quick tile zones), Window::move(), the three geometry signals
and everything connected to them (scene items and repaints, effects including two JavaScript ones,
the org_kde_plasma_window geometry and client_geometry events to every window-management client,
the decoration hover update). A 4000 Hz mouse makes that 4000 times a second, while a 240 Hz output
shows 240 of the positions. In a VM guest stressed with 8000 events/s, kwin_wayland used ~25 s of
CPU per 32 s of drag and plasmashell ~16.5 s (2026-10-17).

The move filter now hands the position to Window::queueInteractiveMoveResize(). The first position
after a frame started is applied at once, as before (v2, 2026-10-17: with every position held for the
frame, a slow drag in a nested desktop reached the screen 1-1.6 ms later at p50); later ones until the
next frame starts are held back, only the latest position and modifiers kept, and applied through
updateInteractiveMoveResize() when that frame starts: the compositor emits the new signal aboutToPrepareFrame() at the start of
composite(), after prepareNewFrame(), so the move is part of that frame and does not schedule
another one on that render loop. Queuing a position asks the window's surface item for a frame
(Item::scheduleFrame()), at the time the moved geometry would have asked for it, so on the outputs
the window is on, frames are neither later nor fewer than before, and each frame shows the latest
position, as before (an output the window enters is scheduled when the position is applied). This
is what Mutter does (META_LATER_BEFORE_REDRAW in meta-window-drag.c).

The latest position is always applied when the move ends (finishInteractiveMoveResize() without
cancel: button release, touch up, Enter, and the other paths that end a move), and before a key is
handled during a move. A direct updateInteractiveMoveResize() call drops the queued position, so a
stale position cannot move the window back. Resizes, a stopped compositor and a window without a
surface item keep the immediate path. Pointer, touch and tablet motion use the queue.

KWIN_MOVE_COALESCE=0 restores the immediate path. Autotests that check a move synchronously right
after Test::pointerMotion() need to wait for a frame (QTRY_COMPARE), see
findings/kwin-move-per-event-2026-10-17.md.


A host KWin keeps its wrapper's environment from login: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kwin-move-coalesce holding
0 or 1 overrides the variable while it exists, read at most once a second (an A/B in a running session).
